{"entity": "OpenUBMC", "type": "organization", "description": "OpenUBMC is an open-source community project and organization dedicated to the development and deployment of Baseboard Management Controller (BMC) software, serving as a foundational platform for hardware and storage system management. It functions as a comprehensive software framework that enables white-label customization and provides a unified interface for managing diverse hardware components, particularly within data center and enterprise environments. Central to its architecture is a modular BMC firmware implementation that supports device discovery via SSDP (Simple Service Discovery Protocol), facilitating seamless network integration and remote access. The platform includes advanced capabilities such as SFF VPD (Smart Fan and Drive Parameter) integration, allowing it to read SSD base information directly from hardware and map it into a hierarchical resource tree for enhanced system visibility.\n\nOpenUBMC supports standardized system telemetry and diagnostics through a Configuration Service Request (CSR) mechanism, enabling consistent monitoring and configuration across managed devices. It also incorporates a structured logging system that records detailed operational data, critical for troubleshooting, auditing, and system maintenance. The platform includes a RAID southbound component driver, contributing to the standardization of storage management within BMC-enabled systems. Additionally, OpenUBMC offers observability solutions enhanced by integrated visualization backends, supporting real-time monitoring and performance analysis.\n\nSecurity is a core priority, with the platform supporting dual signing algorithms — RSA and SM2 — ensuring robust authentication and integrity verification for firmware updates and system operations. It also features DDNS (Dynamic DNS) functionality, improving remote accessibility and manageability of systems across distributed networks. The project is actively maintained through an open-source model, with an official website at https://www.openubmc.cn/ serving as a central hub for documentation, source code access, and community collaboration. While OpenUBMC is primarily recognized for its BMC management tools, firmware, and system integration features, it also provides comprehensive documentation on product development and multi-level customization, supporting both enterprise and developer use cases.<SEP>OpenUBMC is the open-source organization hosting the hardware-sig repositories.<SEP>OpenUBMC is an open-source BMC firmware platform used for managing system hardware and services."}
{"entity": "upgrade_fail", "type": "concept", "description": "The upgrade_fail concept indicates a failed system upgrade operation on a specified node."}
{"entity": "Upgrade Failure", "type": "event", "description": "Upgrade Failure refers to the unsuccessful completion of a system upgrade process, representing the condition that triggers the rollback mechanism when the intended migration does not proceed as expected. This outcome occurs when key system checks—such as operating system compatibility—are not satisfied, or when unresolved software conflicts prevent progress. Specifically, Upgrade Failure has been documented in multiple contexts, including attempts to upgrade from CentOS 7.9 to OpenEuler 22.03 SP1 and from OpenEuler 22.03 SP1 to SP4. In each case, the failure stems from technical obstacles, such as dependency issues, package conflicts, and missing commands during critical stages of the process—most notably during the third step of the upgrade workflow. The failure is not limited to a single phase but encompasses the entire upgrade lifecycle, indicating that the system cannot successfully transition from the base operating system to the target version due to persistent integration challenges. The event is characterized by an inability to complete migration tasks, resulting in a non-functional or unstable state that necessitates intervention. While the core nature of Upgrade Failure remains consistent—its manifestation as an unsuccessful upgrade process—it is observed in distinct technical scenarios involving different source and target OS versions, suggesting variations in root causes across deployments. Nonetheless, the overarching theme is the breakdown in system consistency due to unresolved dependencies and compatibility barriers.<SEP>Upgrade Failure is an event that occurs when a firmware update process fails due to missing configuration, key mismatch, or certificate issues."}
